message.content:
{
  "133": {
    "document_id": "133",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry's statement attacks the administration's 'mismanaged and misguided debacle of a ports deal' in light of the Israel boycott revelation. Like the codebook's negative example of Kerry on the Dubai ports collapse, it is pure criticism and position-taking with no claimed achievement or delivered benefit."
  },
  "214": {
    "document_id": "214",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The text is a Pearl Harbor remembrance honoring those who served. It falls squarely within the negative clarification's exclusion of ceremonial content—commemorations and honoring service members—with no claim of any government achievement."
  },
  "299": {
    "document_id": "299",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Alexander receives a 'Hero of the Taxpayer' award from an outside group and touts his voting record ('I was proud to side with taxpayers on key votes'). This is ceremonial/third-party recognition plus position-taking; per the negative clarification, no concrete achieved government outcome (funds, enacted provision, agency decision) is claimed as his achievement."
  },
  "490": {
    "document_id": "490",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Webb's statement responds to the NIE on Iran by criticizing the Bush administration's 'refusal to pursue constructive dialogue with our adversaries.' It is pure criticism/position-taking on foreign policy with no claimed accomplishment or benefit delivered."
  },
  "630": {
    "document_id": "630",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd comments on the Connecticut impact of Bush's proposed budget, praising Connecticut manufacturers and urging support for two additional submarines per year. This is advocacy for future funding and position-taking on a pending budget; no achieved benefit or delivered funding is claimed as his doing."
  },
  "721": {
    "document_id": "721",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid salutes the Iraqi elections, praises troops, and criticizes the president for lacking a plan. This is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration with no claimed achievement or delivered benefit, excluded under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "773": {
    "document_id": "773",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release reports Senate passage of a resolution honoring the late Senator Eagleton. Honoring a former official is ceremonial content excluded by negative clarification (4); although a resolution passed, it delivers no benefit claimed as the senator's policy achievement—it is a tribute."
  },
  "1060": {
    "document_id": "1060",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While Nelson is partly a supporter of the S-CHIP bill ('joined 66 of his colleagues'), the release headline and text tout that the passed legislation 'includes an amendment co-sponsored by Senator Nelson' and that 'Nelson has long pushed for such legislation.' Under clarification (3), adoption of the senator's own amendment/provision counts even if final enactment is pending, and the release frames the inclusion of his small-business health care amendment as his achievement. The boundary rule (support plus an achieved provision) supports 1."
  },
  "1086": {
    "document_id": "1086",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dayton 'applauded the Senate's passage' of $1 billion in LIHEAP funding and expressed disappointment it was insufficient; his own four cosponsored amendments 'were defeated.' Per negative clarification (3), applauding a chamber action in which he claims no causal role is not credit-claiming, and his defeated amendments represent no achieved benefit. No causal link between his efforts and the funding is asserted."
  },
  "1155": {
    "document_id": "1155",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the statement is largely forward-looking and frames progress collectively, Leahy explicitly notes 'the unanimous adoption of the Leahy Hatch medical criteria amendment' as an achieved milestone through which 'this committee reached consensus on the proper standards,' and notes he chaired the first hearing. Under clarification (3), adoption of the senator's own amendment counts as credit-claiming even while final enactment is pending, and the boundary rule provides that a release combining future advocacy with credit for an achieved outcome is coded 1. The ambiguity is the collective 'we' framing, but the amendment bears his name and is presented as an accomplishment."
  },
  "1313": {
    "document_id": "1313",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman expresses frustration that the House will not take up the D.C. voting rights bill, commends Utah's efforts, and hopes for future action. The bill has not passed—no benefit has materialized—and the release is advocacy/position-taking about pending legislation, excluded under negative clarification (2)."
  },
  "1827": {
    "document_id": "1827",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline announces that provisions of 'Sen. Dick Lugar's Good Samaritan Hunger Relief Act will be included in the Grassley-Baucus tax relief package.' This is the adoption/inclusion of the senator's own provision in legislation, framed as his achievement even though final enactment remains pending—squarely within clarification (3). Although Lugar thanks Grassley and Baucus, the release is structured around his provision being secured into the package, parallel to the positive 'Nelson amendment' example."
  },
  "2003": {
    "document_id": "2003",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Clinton announces the Air Force decision to place a Predator squadron in New York, but she claims no causal role: she 'hailed' the decision as 'a testament to New York's Air National Guard capability' and says 'I am pleased that the Air Force recognizes that New York can support this additional mission.' The release notes no request, intervention, or advocacy on her part. This mirrors the negative example in which Clinton 'welcomes' a favorable decision made by others; under negative clarification (3), welcoming/applauding decisions without a claimed causal role is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "2143": {
    "document_id": "2143",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Murray's statement mourns the passing of former state party chair Karen Marchioro. This is a condolence/memorial statement, which the negative clarification (4) expressly excludes as ceremonial content. No government action or benefit is claimed."
  },
  "3047": {
    "document_id": "3047",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces that USDA will purchase up to 80 million pounds of potatoes and explicitly notes that 'On March 15, 2005, the Idaho Congressional Delegation wrote the Secretary requesting this action.' This claims the delegation's intervention prompted a favorable agency decision benefiting Idaho—clarification (4) covers claims that a letter/request prompted an executive agency to act, and the definition expressly allows shared credit as a delegation member."
  },
  "3201": {
    "document_id": "3201",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces USDA's disaster designation for most of Missouri and the release notes he 'worked with USDA officials to expedite the approval'; it also references his earlier announcement of $1,078,500 in ECP funds for Missouri. This fits clarification (1) (announcement of a disaster designation flowing to his state, noting his advocacy/intervention) and (4) (his intervention prompting agency action)—a claimed causal link between his efforts and a realized federal benefit."
  },
  "3914": {
    "document_id": "3914",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Frist credits 'Republican economic policies' for job growth while attacking Democrats. Any credit is assigned to the party collectively, not to Frist personally, which negative clarification (5) excludes. The statement is partisan position-taking on economic statistics with no claimed personal achievement."
  },
  "3950": {
    "document_id": "3950",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Harkin expresses hope that a new Iowa diabetes facility will help residents, but claims no role in securing it. The release describes him as 'the Senate leader in federal efforts to promote health and wellness'—a generic leadership claim unconnected to a specific achieved outcome, excluded by negative clarification (6)—and notes he authored the HeLP America Act without any claim it was enacted (bill introduction/authorship of pending legislation is excluded under negative clarification (2))."
  },
  "4124": {
    "document_id": "4124",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy commends President Bush's call for comprehensive immigration reform and urges turning 'talk into action.' This is advocacy for future legislation and position-taking; per negative clarification (2) and (3), commending others and pressing for pending measures with no achieved benefit is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4344": {
    "document_id": "4344",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar announces he has joined a bipartisan group supporting the Senate-White House PATRIOT Act compromise and says he is 'pleased the compromise includes provisions shielding most public libraries.' He claims no causal role in producing the compromise or those provisions—he is a supporter expressing approval. This is position-taking on a negotiated deal, excluded under negative clarifications (1) and (3)."
  }
}

{
  "50": {
    "document_id": "50",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement attacks the Bush administration's 'head in the sand' position on global warming. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure position-taking and criticism of the administration with no claimed achievement, delivered benefit, or causal role for the senator."
  },
  "57": {
    "document_id": "57",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy introduces the Human Cloning Ban and Stem Cell Research Protection Act and argues for its passage. Under negative clarification (2), bill introductions and advocacy for pending measures are excluded; no funds, decisions, or benefits have been delivered. This document mirrors the codebook's negative example exactly."
  },
  "89": {
    "document_id": "89",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dorgan announces the federal designation of Bismarck State College as the National Power Plant Operations Technology and Educational Center, a favorable federal designation flowing to his state framed as his own announcement (clarification 1). He also notes he 'authored legislation, which was signed into law, that created the National Center designation,' an explicit claim of authorship of the enacted measure producing the benefit (clarification 2)."
  },
  "240": {
    "document_id": "240",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Ensign praises the Senate's confirmation of Chertoff and says he looks forward to working with him. He claims no role in the confirmation and no benefit is delivered; this is pure position-taking under negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "453": {
    "document_id": "453",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Nelson explains his votes against two Iraq withdrawal resolutions and states his preference for measurable goals over timetables. Under negative clarification (1), explanations of votes and stances on issues with no claimed achievement are excluded."
  },
  "597": {
    "document_id": "597",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lugar presents the Dick Lugar Health and Fitness Award to community honorees at his annual run/walk event. Under negative clarification (4), ceremonial content—bestowing awards on others and honoring constituents—is excluded. No government benefit is claimed."
  },
  "656": {
    "document_id": "656",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry's statement on the collapse of the Dubai Ports deal attacks the Bush administration's 'incompetence' on port security. It claims no accomplishment or benefit attributable to him—pure criticism/position-taking under negative clarification (1). This mirrors the codebook's negative example."
  },
  "918": {
    "document_id": "918",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard announces a town meeting in Greeley. Under negative clarification (4), town meeting announcements are excluded as administrative/ceremonial content; no benefit or achievement is claimed."
  },
  "967": {
    "document_id": "967",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Headlined 'SHELBY ANNOUNCES OVER 3 MILLION FOR TALLADEGA,' the release announces HHS releasing $3,305,732 for Head Start programs in Alabama, framed as Shelby's own announcement and noting his membership on the Senate Appropriations Committee. This fits clarification (1): an announcement of federal money flowing to the senator's state framed as the senator's own announcement, with his appropriations role noted."
  },
  "1021": {
    "document_id": "1021",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Headlined 'SENS. WYDEN, SMITH GAIN FUNDING APPROVAL,' the release announces the Senate Appropriations Committee has approved funding for the Pacific Coast Salmon Recovery Fund ($13 million for Oregon) and $2 million for OSU ocean research, framed as the senators' achievement. Under clarifications (1) and (3), secured provisions approved in committee count even though final enactment is pending—paralleling the Talent positive example."
  },
  "1957": {
    "document_id": "1957",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dodd and Lieberman announce that Bradley International Airport will receive a $2,250,000 federal grant for runway/taxiway improvements. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal money flowing to the senators' state framed as the senators' own announcement are included as credit-claiming; the release is structured around their announcement of the delivered grant."
  },
  "2318": {
    "document_id": "2318",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the letter urges state-level action, Durbin explicitly states: 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding.' These are explicit first-person claims of responsibility for already-achieved favorable federal actions (clarifications 2 and 5). The boundary rule directs that a release both advocating future action and claiming credit for achieved outcomes is coded 1. This mirrors the codebook's positive example."
  },
  "2807": {
    "document_id": "2807",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lincoln 'applauds' and helps recognize Arkansas Women in Agriculture month—ceremonial recognition content excluded under negative clarification (4). The closing biographical note that she 'played a key role in the 2002 farm bill debate' is a generic leadership claim tied to a debate rather than a specific achieved outcome attributable to her (negative clarification 6); no funds, provisions, or decisions are claimed as her achievement."
  },
  "3482": {
    "document_id": "3482",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Mikulski and Cardin announce that Allegany County has been awarded a $294,980 federal SAFER grant, framed as the senators' own announcement of federal funds flowing to their state (clarification 1). The release further notes Mikulski's seat on the Homeland Security Appropriations Subcommittee that funds the program and that she 'fights each year to increase its federal funding,' reinforcing the implied causal link between her efforts and the delivered grant."
  },
  "3577": {
    "document_id": "3577",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This is primarily a hearing statement criticizing FEMA and outlining future issues (position-taking), but Sarbanes makes an explicit first-person claim about an already-achieved legislative outcome: 'the reforms that I worked with Senator Bunning and others on the Committee to formulate and move through'—reforms Congress enacted (statutory deadlines are referenced) and which he calls 'quite important indeed.' Under clarifications (2) and (5) (explicit first-person claims; retrospective claims regarding past legislation enacted) and the boundary rule (a release that both advocates future action and claims credit for an achieved outcome is coded 1), this qualifies. I acknowledge difficulty: the credit is shared with Bunning and the committee, the release is not structured around the claim, and the reforms' benefits remain unrealized because FEMA has not implemented them—but the explicit personal claim of a role in enacted legislation tips it to 1."
  },
  "3659": {
    "document_id": "3659",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Clinton 'welcomes' a NIOSH advisory board recommendation and urges the HHS Secretary to act on it, claiming no role in producing the recommendation. Under negative clarification (3), welcoming or applauding others' decisions without a claimed causal role is excluded; the only forward action is future advocacy. This mirrors the codebook's negative example."
  },
  "3687": {
    "document_id": "3687",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Bingaman announces that New Mexico State University has been awarded $599,691 in USDA funds for food safety research. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal money flowing to the senator's state framed as the senator's own announcement are included; the grant is represented as already awarded, not merely sought."
  },
  "4208": {
    "document_id": "4208",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dorgan and Lott announce they will introduce the Media Ownership Act of 2007 to halt the FCC's easing of ownership rules. Under negative clarification (2), bill introductions and advocacy for pending measures where no benefit has yet materialized are excluded."
  },
  "4585": {
    "document_id": "4585",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Schumer announces that two Westchester County fire departments have received $134,004 in federal fire grants, framed as his own announcement of federal funds delivered to his state (clarification 1). The release further notes that 'Schumer has Pushed for Additional Funding' for the program and that he was 'among the first senators' to recognize the need for it, asserting an advocacy role behind the delivered benefit."
  },
  "4771": {
    "document_id": "4771",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While framed around a meeting, the release states that in 2005 Clinton 'played a key role along with SMAC in preventing the removal of four C-130 Aircraft' and references the BRAC Commission's 2005 decision to keep the aircraft at Stratton—an achieved outcome beneficial to her constituents. This is an explicit retrospective credit claim (clarification 2 lists 'played a key role'; clarification 5 covers previously delivered benefits), and the boundary rule directs coding 1 when a release combines ongoing advocacy with a claim of credit for an outcome already achieved."
  }
}

{
  "817": {
    "document_id": "817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Collins testified at a hearing in support of her pending Social Security Fairness Act and discussed the problem it would address. Under negative clarification (2), hearings, testimony, and advocacy for pending measures where no benefit has yet materialized are excluded. The bill has not passed; no outcome has been achieved."
  },
  "871": {
    "document_id": "871",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar questioned Interior Secretary Norton about proposed budget cuts to PILT, LWCF, and Park Service rules, criticizing the administration. This is oversight and criticism of the administration—pure position-taking under negative clarification (1). No funds were secured and no favorable decision is claimed as resulting from his efforts; his letter on the Park Service rules is advocacy, not an achieved outcome."
  },
  "1118": {
    "document_id": "1118",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Carper's statement explains his vote against the constitutional amendment banning gay marriage, arguing that existing state laws are working. Under negative clarification (1), explanations of votes and statements of opinion are pure position-taking. No achievement, funding, or delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1177": {
    "document_id": "1177",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Pryor and Chambliss 'called on' the Air Force to reimburse unpaid subcontractors and announced they 'would continue to mount pressure' and pursue a legislative fix. Under negative clarification (2), advocacy, requests, and pressure aimed at future action where no benefit has yet materialized are excluded. The Air Force has not yet acted; nothing has been achieved."
  },
  "1230": {
    "document_id": "1230",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although Hatch and Harkin 'welcome' the FDA's publication of GMP regulations, the release claims a causal role for them: they were 'the principal authors' of DSHEA (an enacted 1994 law), 'which gave the FDA the authority to write GMP guidelines,' and 'they have been pushing the FDA to publish GMPs for more than a decade.' Hatch adds, 'That's why we included GMPs in DSHEA.' This falls under clarification (4) (claiming the senators' pressure prompted an agency to act) and clarification (5) (retrospective claim of a previously enacted legislative achievement), distinguishing it from the codebook's Clinton 'welcoming' negative example where no causal role was claimed."
  },
  "1320": {
    "document_id": "1320",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Headlined 'SEN. HUTCHISON SECURES DROUGHT, WILDFIRE RELIEF FOR TEXAS,' the release announces passage of an emergency appropriations bill containing $3 billion in disaster relief, with Hutchison quoted: 'I am pleased to help secure this funding.' This is an explicit first-person claim of responsibility for delivered federal funds under clarification (1) and (2)."
  },
  "1747": {
    "document_id": "1747",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The Senate 'has approved legislation by Sen. Robert C. Byrd' to boost funding for the National Guard Youth ChalleNGe program; 'Byrd's provision' in the Defense Authorization Bill passed the Senate 98-0. Under clarification (3), passage of the senator's own provision—framed here as Byrd's achievement—counts even though the bill still must go to conference. The release also notes retrospectively that Byrd 'worked with the National Guard Bureau to create this program in 1991,' an additional retrospective credit claim under clarification (5)."
  },
  "1862": {
    "document_id": "1862",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Baucus and Tester announce $12.5 million for Butte military projects included in the Defense Appropriations Bill passed by the Senate Appropriations Committee, stating they 'have delivered on their promise to secure federal funding' and 'were proud to go to bat for these dollars.' This is an explicit first-person claim of securing federal funds for their state (clarification (1) and (2)), with the funding approved in committee—an achieved step analogous to the Talent positive example."
  },
  "2026": {
    "document_id": "2026",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline announces 'Senate Judiciary Panel Passes Leahy Specter Data Privacy And Security Bill,' describing it as legislation 'introduced by Chairman Patrick Leahy' and repeatedly calling it 'the Leahy Specter' bill. Committee passage of the senator's own bill is framed as his legislative achievement. Under clarification (3), passage/adoption of the senator's own bill counts as credit-claiming even though final enactment is still pending (the bill 'now moves to the full Senate'). The residual ambiguity is whether committee (rather than floor) passage suffices, but the release's structure—headline claiming passage of the eponymous bill with Leahy's statement touting it—claims the milestone as his own."
  },
  "2627": {
    "document_id": "2627",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This matches the codebook's paradigmatic positive example: Talent announces 'the Senate has approved his request for $1.5 million' for a University of Missouri facility—an explicit first-person claim of securing federal funds for a home-state institution (clarification (1) and (2))."
  },
  "2675": {
    "document_id": "2675",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd, Lieberman, and Simmons met with BRAC commissioners to argue against closing Sub Base New London. The BRAC decision is still pending—'until we reach the finish line and hopefully prevail our work isn't done.' Under negative clarification (2), meetings and advocacy for a pending future decision with no achieved benefit are excluded. No outcome is claimed as achieved."
  },
  "3271": {
    "document_id": "3271",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces 'unanimous Senate passage of legislation... authored by Idaho Senator Mike Crapo,' describing him as 'the Senate leader in prompting reforms' who 'has led the Senate Banking Committee.' This is Senate passage of the senator's own authored bill framed as his achievement, squarely within clarification (3), which applies even though final enactment is pending (the measure now goes to the House)."
  },
  "3477": {
    "document_id": "3477",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release announces the Senate's confirmation of Carol Dinkins (a Texan) to chair the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board and quotes Cornyn praising her and saying he 'stand[s] prepared to work with the board.' Although it notes Cornyn chaired the nominations hearing, he makes no claim that he caused or secured the appointment—no request, recommendation, or intervention is asserted. The essential claimed causal link between the senator's efforts and the outcome is absent, so this is an informational announcement rather than credit-claiming (contrast with document 3729, where Grassley explicitly notes he nominated the appointee)."
  },
  "3501": {
    "document_id": "3501",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Tester expresses disappointment at the Air Force's decision to eliminate missiles at Malmstrom and says 'I will continue to fight' and 'work toward getting another mission.' This is a statement of position and a promise of future advocacy (negative clarification (1) and (2)); no achieved benefit or favorable decision is claimed."
  },
  "3534": {
    "document_id": "3534",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Domenici 'credited President Bush' for the gasoline price plan and urged Congress to do more. Credit is assigned entirely to the President, not to Domenici himself; he claims no causal role in the plan and announces no achieved benefit attributable to his efforts. This is position-taking/applauding another's action under negative clarification (3) and (5)."
  },
  "3560": {
    "document_id": "3560",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The Arkansas delegation announces HUD grants to state housing agencies, but the senators explicitly assign the causal role to others: Lincoln 'applaud[s] the public housing agencies in Arkansas that sought out these federal funds,' and Berry 'commend[s] all of the grant recipients for securing these federal funds.' Pryor, Snyder, Boozman, and Ross merely praise the programs or express pride in announcing them. Under negative clarification (3), applauding/commending others for awards in which the senator claims no causal role is excluded; no senator claims to have secured, requested, or advocated for these specific funds, so the essential claimed causal link is missing. I acknowledge ambiguity because the release is framed as the delegation's own announcement of federal money (clarification (1)), but the explicit crediting of the recipients controls here."
  },
  "3729": {
    "document_id": "3729",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Grassley announces that President Bush named Matt Dummermuth interim U.S. Attorney for the Northern District of Iowa, and the release notes 'Dummermuth was one of three names Grassley nominated to fill the opening.' This is an achieved appointment to an Iowa office explicitly linked to Grassley's own recommendation—an implied causal claim of the senator's role in delivering the appointment under clarification (1) (appointments flowing to the senator's state with the senator's advocacy noted)."
  },
  "3744": {
    "document_id": "3744",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces the CDC's decision to establish a Hawaii field station, a federal facility flowing to his state framed as his own announcement (clarification (1)). Additionally, the release makes a retrospective credit claim: 'Several years ago the [CDC] at Senator Inouye's request established a small occupational health center at the University of Hawaii at Hilo'—a previously delivered benefit explicitly attributed to his request (clarifications (4) and (5)). Even though the new station's funding is not final, the boundary rule applies: the release both advocates future action and claims credit for an already-achieved outcome, so it is coded 1."
  },
  "3845": {
    "document_id": "3845",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Bayh's statement criticizes OPEC and promotes his pending energy bill ('I have offered an energy plan... When Congress returns... they should pass my plan'). This is position-taking plus advocacy for legislation that has not passed—no achieved outcome is claimed. Negative clarification (1) and (2) apply; being 'lead sponsor' of a pending bill is not an achievement under the boundary rule."
  },
  "4531": {
    "document_id": "4531",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Thune and Clinton announce the introduction of legislation to extend child care benefits for families of fallen soldiers. Under negative clarification (2), bill introductions are excluded. The references to past bills they 'co sponsored,' 'supported,' or 'championed' describe sponsorship and advocacy, not enacted achievements, so no realized benefit is claimed."
  }
}

{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce they have reached a compromise agreement on a plan to strengthen FEMA, with provisions to be included in the Collins-Lieberman bill that is only being introduced today. Under negative clarification (2), bill introductions and agreements on pending measures where no benefit has yet materialized are excluded; there is no adopted provision (clarification 3 requires adoption or passage, not introduction) and no realized government action or benefit for which credit is claimed."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the column describes and praises the U.S. Marshals Service, the senator makes an explicit first-person claim: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is a direct claim of responsibility for secured appropriations, squarely within clarification (2) (explicit first-person claims such as 'I helped secure')."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Most of the release is position-taking at a chamber event, but Bond states: 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This is a personal causal claim (his co-auth
